{
  "requirement": "Preserves the selector and a diagnostic explaining the syntax error.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct SelectorError {\n    pub selector: String,\n    pub message: String,\n}\n\nimpl SelectorError {\n    pub fn new(selector: &str, message: impl Into<String>) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for SelectorError;\nimpl Debug for SelectorError;\nimpl Display for SelectorError;\nimpl Error for SelectorError;\nimpl PartialEq for SelectorError;\nimpl Eq for SelectorError;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.outlines.SelectorError` says the error preserves the selector and a diagnostic message. It also gives a signature: public `selector` and `message` fields, `new`, `#[non_exhaustive]`, and the trait impls Clone, Debug, Display, Error, PartialEq and Eq.\n\nThe marked test `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` loops over seven invalid selectors: `fn`, `fn[run`, `[run]`, `fn[run]#0`, `use@123`, `fn[run]x` and `a//b`. For each one it checks the following:\n- `error.selector == invalid`, so the selector is preserved.\n- `!error.message.is_empty()`, so a diagnostic is present.\n- `error.clone() == SelectorError::new(invalid, &error.message)`. This exercises `Clone`, `PartialEq`, and `new` taking `&str` and `impl Into<String>`. `new` is also checked for populating the fields correctly.\n- `&dyn std::error::Error` coercion, which checks the `Error` impl.\n- `to_string()` contains both the selector and the message, which checks `Display`.\n\n`Debug` and `Eq` are checked at compile time. The same test calls `unwrap()` and `unwrap_err()` on the parse results, which needs `Debug` on both the error and the success type. A missing `Eq` impl would break compilation of the derive-based `ResolveError`, which derives `Eq`.\n\nThe second marked test, `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`, checks the public export inventory. It does this by compiling the Habeas consumer against the SDK and comparing rustdoc paths and item kinds to the specified exports.\n\nThe remaining details are the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute and the negative case of `PartialEq`. The attribute is a declarative item with no runtime behaviour. The derived `PartialEq` has no conditional or transformation of its own. The text names no further boundary or failure case for either. The Display wording is unspecified, so asserting that it contains both the selector and the message is sufficient. I found no gap and no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "f65a79860c11538c0d1ddd31f000335aacb8b48d92b940aa758fb0218c520eba",
